From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 03:33:40 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] drm/asahi: g16: expose g16_idle_power_off in sysfs and switch
 it at runtime

The idle core power-off knob was a module! parameter. rust/macros/module.rs
registers every such parameter with perm 0 ("Will not appear in sysfs"),
and rust/kernel/module_param.rs stores it in a SetOnce with no get op, so
/sys/module/asahi/parameters/g16_idle_power_off never existed: the knob
could only be set on the kernel command line or in modprobe.d, and nothing
could read it back on a running system. A feature check that read sysfs
reported FAIL on 1.608 for that reason alone.

Declare it with its own kernel_param entry instead, as the M3 backend
does for m3_retire_mmio: perm 0644, AtomicU32 storage, a get op, and set
storing min(level, 2). modinfo strings keep the module! spelling, so
`modinfo asahi` still lists it.

The level at probe still selects the initdata (Globals +0x30) and, for 2,
the SGX +0xd06030 gating step; both now come from one read kept in
InitData. A later write changes whether Globals +0x30 is 0 or 1. The
power service publishes the change, before the next queue or doorbell
publication, the same way it publishes the performance selectors in the
same shared Globals object. The journal records each change ("M4 power:
idle core power-off a -> b"), and the debugfs stats gain a line
"idle_power_off: published P requested R boot_level L changes N".

This lets a running system be A/B tested without a reboot, and any reboot
returns the knob to its boot value. The default stays 0. Whether the 25G83
firmware honours +0x30 written after boot is not qualified on T8132;
a measurement on the J604 decides it, and a later change flips the default
if it passes.

Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5.5
